@title What Eighteen Months of Four-Day Weeks Did to Our Support Team
@subtitle Halden Cartography blog
@author Priscilla Adeyemi-Koh, Head of Customer Support
@date 2026-10

On 2026-10-02 we made it official: our support team is staying on a four-day week. The decision closed an eighteen-month pilot that started on 2025-04-07, and I want to write down what we learned while it is still fresh, including the parts that did not go smoothly.

## Why we tried it

In 2024 our support team lost 21 percent of its people. For a team of 23, that means roughly one departure every couple of months, each one taking customer context, product knowledge and a piece of the team's confidence out the door with them.

When we read the exit interviews, one reason came up more than any other: burnout. Not pay, not career growth, not the tooling. People were tired. Support work is relentless in a quiet way. The queue never empties, every ticket is someone's bad day, and the satisfaction of closing one is immediately replaced by the next.

I had read the research on shorter weeks and was skeptical. Most of it came from knowledge-work teams who could batch their deep work. Support is different: customers do not care what day it is. But I was more worried about losing another fifth of the team than about trying something new, so we designed a pilot.

## How the schedule works

The core of it is simple. Everyone works Monday to Thursday, 9 hours a day. That is 36 hours a week, and everyone is paid exactly what they were paid before.

The hard part was Friday. Our customers still send tickets on Fridays, and some of them are urgent. So we built a rotating on-call group of 4 people who cover Friday. Those four take the following Monday off instead, so they still get a three-day weekend, just shifted. The rotation means nobody is stuck on Fridays for long, and everyone understands what Friday coverage feels like.

We wrote the coverage rules down before the first week: who is on, what counts as urgent, what can wait for Monday, and how to escalate. I will come back to why that mattered.

## What happened to the numbers

I expected our customer metrics to slip a little, and I was prepared to argue that a small slip was worth it for the team. That argument turned out to be unnecessary.

Our median first response time fell from 3.4 hours to 2.9 hours. Customer satisfaction rose from 4.3 to 4.5 out of 5. I still do not fully understand the response time improvement, but my best guess is that rested people triage faster and make fewer mistakes that bounce back as follow-up tickets.

The team numbers moved even more. Annual attrition fell from 21 percent to 9 percent. Sick days fell by 31 percent. Those two numbers are the ones I think about most, because they represent people who stayed, and days people were well enough to be here.

I want to be careful not to claim too much. Eighteen months is long enough to see a pattern but not long enough to rule out everything else that changed in that time. Still, every number we cared about moved in the right direction, and none moved in the wrong one.

## What got harder

Not everything improved, and pretending otherwise would not help anyone thinking about doing this.

Thursday evenings became a pressure point. With a long weekend ahead, handoffs to the Friday group had to be clean, and in the early months they often were not. Tickets got passed along without enough context, and the on-call group spent their Fridays reconstructing what had happened. We got better at it, but it took deliberate effort.

Two of our enterprise customers wanted guaranteed Friday phone support, not just ticket coverage. That was a fair request and our rotation could not meet it on its own, so we hired 2 more people to cover it. That is a real cost, and I think it is worth being honest that the four-day week was not free for us.

And a few people found 9-hour days tiring, especially in winter, when the day starts and ends in the dark. For them the long weekend did not fully make up for the long days. We have talked about it openly, and I do not think we have a perfect answer yet.

## If you try it

If you lead a support team and you are curious about this, here is what I would tell you.

1. **Pilot for at least 6 months.** The first few weeks feel great because everything is new. The real problems, like the Thursday handoffs and the winter fatigue, only show up later.
2. **Write the coverage rules before the first week.** Decide who covers what, what counts as urgent and how escalation works before anyone starts. Arguing about it mid-pilot is far harder.
3. **Measure customer metrics alongside team morale.** If you only track how the team feels, you will not be able to defend the change. If you only track customer numbers, you will miss the reason you did it.

Eighteen months ago I was not sure this would work. Today I cannot imagine asking the team to go back.
